{"table":"nodes","ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.source_fixed_strip_application","record":{"id":"scalar.source_fixed_strip_application","kind":"claim","label":"Fixed-strip mesh limit with every analytic input supplied","status":"unverified","source_id":"scalar-r2-26-source-contact-assembly","locator":"Variation/ContactAssembly.lean:61–69; IsDiffusion.finite_gaussian_of_contact, concrete fixed-strip application","statement":"For every W,gamma,X with IsMinimizer W gamma and IsDiffusion W gamma X, and every fixed T with 0<T<1, eventually for all N and all j<N the normalizer is positive, and shiftedCoefficientSum W gamma T N tends as N→∞ to ∫₀ᵀE curvature(t,X_t)dt.","assumption_ids":["assumptions.value_minimizer","assumptions.value_diffusion"],"evidence_ids":[],"citation_ids":["scalar-r2-13-cite-fixed-strip-application"],"boundary":"The supplier application unpacks constants from sourceStripRegularity and restricts the unit curvature moment to Icc 0 T. It does not take these as new caller premises, assert positivity at every mesh, give a rate, or take a diagonal or uniform terminal limit. Authored correspondence to inspected frozen source; unverified status is retained. No fresh Lean build, elaborated dependency extraction, axiom audit, Comparator acceptance, or independent mathematical acceptance is claimed."},"usage":{"record_ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.source_fixed_strip_application","paper_focus":[],"paper_memberships":[{"stage":"long","subgraph_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/support/0.3.1/2f85413164f06365ea0d825befee447650da71d54080d5dd65e78a708891c560","roles":["mapped_claim"]},{"stage":"short","subgraph_ref":"sha256:cfbe950801fd334d8fd957243fa3b1f716ef609148c5f2ff97d44f9a6443d82c/papers/short.json/support/0.3.1/77f0cf29ab577b42d8caa5b2831ebfb1ca170d4b13c1ef6897cadc8e93c2a834","roles":["recorded_prerequisite"]},{"stage":"expository","subgraph_ref":"sha256:479155de953858e60f1454491ccda2215d88a4c2780e686cdfb8422481c62d9c/papers/expository.json/support/0.3.1/9dd5536bc567fc55efaa864733bf89747cea0268a8a3865dec51ddd8e2a235dd","roles":["recorded_prerequisite"]}],"direct_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/gaussian-expectation-limit","anchor":"gaussian-expectation-limit","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"},{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/formal-scalar","anchor":"formal-scalar","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"}]},"aligned_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/gaussian-expectation-limit","anchor":"gaussian-expectation-limit","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n<a id=\"gaussian-expectation-limit\"></a>\n\n#### 10.2.4 Passing the expectation bound and returning to the value\n\nSince $S^2=1$, $\\|\\widehat S_n\\|=1$ for $n>0$, and every residual\nprojection contracts. The column formula gives\n$|E_{n,j}|\\le1+\\|\\widehat z_{j+1,n}\\|^2$.\nDefine\n$$\nc_n=\\sum_{j<K}(1+\\|\\widehat z_{j+1,n}\\|^2).\n$$\nThen $\\sum_jE_{n,j}+c_n\\ge0$, $\\mathbb E c_n=2K$ exactly, and\n$c_n\\to2K$ almost surely. Fatou's inequality, the finite bound and\n$e_n\\to P_*$ imply\n$$\n\\sum_{j<K}a_jb_{j+1}+2K\n\\le\\liminf_n\\mathbb E\\bigl[\\sum_{j<K}E_{n,j}+c_n\\bigr]\n\\le P_*+2K.\n$$\nCancelling $2K$ proves the claimed predictable-coefficient inequality.\nThe source's constant-limit helper establishes the same nonnegative\ncomparison by clipping at the limiting constant and applying dominated\nconvergence. It shifts to dimensions $n+1$ to avoid dimension zero.\nIt does not apply Fatou directly to the signed energies or presume\nuniform integrability. [Column bound and correction][scalar-empirical-bound];\n[expectation limit and final theorem][scalar-predictable].\n\nFor the Euler construction in §7, take $K=N$, relabel its $Z_{j+1}$ as\n$z_j$, and set $f_j=g_{j+1}$. Each normalized increment depends only\non $z_0,\\ldots,z_j$ and the increments are orthonormal when their raw\nsecond moments are positive. The rounded sign is spin-valued almost\nsurely: its tie event is null by the independent last coordinate.\nThe scalar implementation uses a measurable Bool spin equal to that\nsign almost surely. It follows that\n$$\nS_{T,N}=\\sum_{j=1}^N A_jB_{j+1}\\le P_*.\n$$\nThis finite inequality assumes admissibility, $0\\le T<1$, $N>0$ and\npositive active second moments, but no minimizer or diffusion.\n[Literal scalar specialization][scalar-literal-lower].\n\nAt a minimizer, the separate contact and diffusion arguments establish\nthe fixed-strip inputs and eventual positivity. First take the mesh\nlimit to obtain $I(T)\\le P_*$, then let $T\\uparrow1$. The scalar\nterminal assembly identifies that last limit with $\\mathcal P(\\gamma)$;\ntogether with $P_*\\le\\mathcal P(\\gamma)$ this proves equality.\nThe third scalar endpoint constructs its own diffusion before making\nthis application. These sources therefore supply an independent route\nto the equality, alongside the imported Parisi formula in §6.\n[Contact assembly][scalar-contact-assembly]. The inspection here does\nnot change the research replay outcome in §13.\n","span":{"start_byte":189849,"end_byte":192242,"start_line":4063,"end_line":4114},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=gaussian-expectation-limit","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=gaussian-expectation-limit"},{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/formal-scalar","anchor":"formal-scalar","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n<a id=\"formal-scalar-statement\"></a>\n\n### 12.2 The separate scalar statement\n\nThe second formal statement has three clauses. Given a minimizer and an\nassociated diffusion, it establishes convergence of the finite-spin\nground-state values, a bounded martingale with a spin-valued terminal\nlimit, convergence of the gradient and the value identities. Under the\nsame hypotheses and fixed $0<T<1$, it gives eventual positivity of the\nGaussian normalizers and convergence of the finite coefficient sum.\nThese are the declarations `scalar_value_target` and\n`finite_gaussian_target`. Their caller supplies the same minimizer and\nassociated diffusion. The implementation derives compact-strip bounds\nfrom `sourceStripRegularity` and the unit curvature moment from contact\nfor those objects before applying its fixed-strip helper. Those internal\ninputs are not additional hypotheses of the exported clauses. The\nconvergence clause establishes existence of the finite-spin limit.\n\nThe index match in the Gaussian statement is exact. For\n$\\Delta=T/N$ and $t_j=j\\Delta$, the Euler path uses\n$Z_1,\\ldots,Z_N$ and produces $Y_0,\\ldots,Y_N$.\nThe source normalizer indexed from zero corresponds to manuscript\n$c_1,\\ldots,c_N$; its eventual positivity covers precisely those\nnormalizers. A separate $Z_{N+1}$ performs mean-preserving rounding:\nit enters $B_{N+1}$, not another Euler increment. Thus the finite sum is\n$\\sum_{j=0}^{N}A_jB_{j+1}$ with $A_0=0$, using exactly the displayed\nindex ranges. Total definitions at $N=0$ make no extra mathematical\nclaim about the construction for $N\\ge1$. More explicitly, the\nzero-based formal quantities `coordinate N j`, `rawCoefficient ... j`\nand `normalizer ... j` correspond to $Z_{j+1}$,\n$a(t_j,Y_j)$ and $c_{j+1}$ for $0\\le j<N$. The positivity quantifiers\nare $\\exists N_0\\,\\forall N\\ge N_0\\,\\forall j<N$, simultaneously over\nthe growing mesh, for each already fixed $T$. The extra coordinate\nwith formal index $N$ is $Z_{N+1}$. The formal martingale stops at its\nterminal random variable $U$ for all later times; $U^2=1$ almost surely,\nand the gradient converges to $U$ almost surely and in $L^2$ from below\none. The internal terminal-gradient lemma proves $UX_1=|X_1|$\nwithout requiring $X_1$ to have no atom at zero. That identity is used\ninside the implementation; it is not an extra conjunct of the endpoint. See the [scalar statement][value-type] and its\n[complete dictionary](https://github.com/wcook04/lean-exposition/blob/21b1f9e995c4276fdad02377b378f11541a3d5e9/papers/qaoa-sk/source-review/scalar-value-interface.md).\n\n","span":{"start_byte":241201,"end_byte":243764,"start_line":5199,"end_line":5240},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=formal-scalar","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=formal-scalar"}],"lenses":{"prerequisites":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=scalar.source_fixed_strip_application&assumptions=true","consumers":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=scalar.source_fixed_strip_application&direction=consumers&assumptions=true","direct_passages":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=scalar.source_fixed_strip_application&relation=direct","prerequisite_uses":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=scalar.source_fixed_strip_application&relation=prerequisites"},"relations":[{"family":"arguments","id":"field:7fb6956462d368a50eec1f2fdf54feb9d6c68af1d0368cf34c4710d9bf49d1c8","from":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.source_fixed_strip_application","to":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/assumptions.value_diffusion","kind":"assumption_ids","semantic_class":"explicit_assumption_reference","derived":true,"owner_ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.source_fixed_strip_application","source_field":"assumption_ids"},{"family":"arguments","id":"field:c311bb715e1de52cbaa84c373d39861bd0aa6bad3ff32290c1f3fe1feeb6210f","from":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.source_fixed_strip_application","to":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/assumptions.value_minimizer","kind":"assumption_ids","semantic_class":"explicit_assumption_reference","derived":true,"owner_ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.source_fixed_strip_application","source_field":"assumption_ids"},{"family":"arguments",